![Completed vector route](images/b2/b2f03d3c8081b95f0b3fb456fe16c35431d92e861e46fe0c6de51bdbafc091f3.png)

Construct the route $O\to X\to C$, first parallel to $\mathbf{a}$ and then parallel to $\mathbf{b}$.

Along $OX$, move $5$ squares right and $5$ squares down. A copy of $\mathbf{a}$ moves $2$ squares left and $2$ squares up. Thus $OX=\frac{5}{2}|\mathbf{a}|$ and the segment points in the opposite direction to $\mathbf{a}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{OX}=-\frac{5}{2}\mathbf{a}$.

Along $XC$, move $2$ squares up. A copy of $\mathbf{b}$ moves $2$ squares up. Thus $XC=1|\mathbf{b}|$ and the segment points in the same direction to $\mathbf{b}$. Hence $\overrightarrow{XC}=\mathbf{b}$.

$$
\begin{aligned}
\mathbf{c}&=\overrightarrow{OX}+\overrightarrow{XC}\\&=-\frac{5}{2}\mathbf{a}+\mathbf{b}
\end{aligned}
$$

Since $\mathbf{a}$ and $\mathbf{b}$ are not parallel, their coefficients are unique. Matching coefficients gives $\lambda=-\frac{5}{2}$ and $\mu=1$.

